WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.280
Running a batch: laundry detergent, dish liquid and floor cleaner.

2
00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.780
This is the video for module 2: Production Runs.

3
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:13.320
Formulas are written in percent by weight, so they work for any batch size.

4
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:23.640
To find each ingredient, multiply the batch weight by its percentage. So 8% of a 20 kg batch is 1.60 kg.

5
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.810
Write every weight on a batch sheet before you open a single container.

6
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:35.500
And record the batch as you go, because your next batch starts from this record, not from memory.

7
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:47.440
For example, Chiamaka in Onitsha supplies two laundries. Her LABSA, the sulphonic acid, is 96% strength. She needs 1.60 kg of it.

8
00:00:48.690 --> 00:01:01.360
Caustic soda turns LABSA into a strong cleaner. About 0.118 kg neutralises each kilogram of 96% LABSA, so she needs about 189 g.

9
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:09.460
LABSA varies, so she adds only about 90% at first, then finishes slowly by measuring pH.

10
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:18.860
SLES adds foam. Soda ash softens hard water. CMC stops loosened dirt settling back, which keeps whites white.

11
00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.630
Water makes up the rest, to 20 kg.

12
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.890
Goggles, gloves, apron and long sleeves on. Open the window.

13
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:36.460
Weigh about 12 kg of water into a plastic drum, then pour in the LABSA slowly while stirring with a long paddle.

14
00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:47.050
Dissolve the caustic flakes into cold water in a separate jug, then add it to the drum in portions, waiting between each. The drum gets warm.

15
00:01:48.300 --> 00:02:01.080
After about 90%, test with a pH strip. Keep adding small amounts until the pH is about 7 to 8. If you overshoot, add a very small amount of LABSA, 20 g at a time.

16
00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:09.580
Only now add the soda ash, so it does not fizz. Then CMC, sprinkled slowly, then the SLES, stirred gently.

17
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:16.440
Rest it overnight so the foam rises, check the pH is about 9 to 10, and fill.

18
00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:24.150
LABSA and caustic soda both burn skin and eyes, and they make heat together. Keep your eye-wash water within reach.

19
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:31.870
Dish liquid touches hands for a long time, so it stops at about pH 7, with no soda ash.

20
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:42.930
Salt thickens it, but only up to a point. Past that point it thins again, and you cannot take the salt out. So make a 25% salt solution.

21
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:53.840
For example, Funke in Abeokuta adds 100 g at a time, stirs, and times how long 100 ml takes to flow through a funnel.

22
00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:05.950
The time rises from 4 seconds up to 22 seconds at 500 g. At 600 g it drops to 21. She has passed the peak, so she stops.

23
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.810
She writes on her formula: next time, stop at 500 g and check.

24
00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:27.520
Your label promises 500 ml, but your scale measures weight, and dish liquid is a little heavier than water. So Funke weighs exactly 1 litre: 1,030 g. That is 1.03 g per ml.

25
00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:32.870
So each 500 ml bottle gets 515 g.

26
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:41.590
Her 25 kg batch fills 48 full bottles, so she buys exactly 48 bottles, caps and labels.

27
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.080
The 280 g left over is her retained sample, in case anyone complains.

28
00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:57.420
She zeros the scale with an empty bottle and cap on it, fills each to 515 g, and checks every tenth bottle again.

29
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:03.320
Floor cleaner is a concentrate, so the dose on your label is part of the product.

30
00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:19.520
For example, Rukayat in Ilorin tests her concentrate on four equal patches of a greasy restaurant kitchen floor, using a 5 litre bucket each. 1 in 100 cleans with no film. 1 in 50 is no better and leaves foam.

31
00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:23.740
So she chooses the weakest rung that still cleans well.

32
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:29.740
Her label gives the dose in millilitres, the bucket size, and a warning not to mix with bleach.

33
00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:35.400
The manager can see a 4 litre keg lasts 80 kitchen buckets, which helps her sell it.

34
00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:50.330
To remember. Work from percentages to kilograms. Add caustic in portions and finish by pH. Thicken in measured steps. Fill by weight. And write clear directions with a dose and a no-mixing warning.

35
00:04:51.580 --> 00:05:03.100
Now run a 2 kg laundry test batch with full PPE, thicken and fill a dish test batch by weight, and run your own dilution ladder. Continue with the lessons below.
